{{also|Hammock}}
==English==
[[File:Couple in Hammock.jpg|right|thumb|A couple in a '''hammock'''.]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|es|hamaca}}, from {{der|en|tnq|*hamaka}} (compare {{cog|arw|hamaka}}, {{cog|guc|jama'a}}), from {{der|en|awd-pro|*hamaka}}. Columbus, in the narrative of his first voyage, says: “A great many Indians in canoes came to the ship to-day for the purpose of bartering their cotton, and '''hamacas''', or nets, in which they sleep.”

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæmək/}}
* {{a|Aus}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhæmək/|/ˈhæmɪk/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-hammock.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æmək|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A swinging [[couch]] or [[bed]], usually made of [[net]]ting or [[canvas]] about six feet (1.8 meters) wide, suspended by [[clew]]s or [[cord]]s at the ends.
#* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|edition=2nd|page=7|passage={{...}} the poore ſaylers, who {{...}} commonly get forthwith into their beds (or '''hamackoes''') reſting their tyred bodies {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|US|archaic|outside|dialects}} A piece of land thickly wooded, and usually covered with bushes and vines.

